{"id": "WS-DISC-01", "criterion": "Root detection walks up from the start directory, never past the Git toplevel, and picks the nearest directory with a workspace marker (Cargo.toml with a `workspace` table, package.json with `workspaces`, pnpm-workspace.yaml, a .moon directory, or a callisto.toml); with no marker it picks the outermost directory with a package manifest (Cargo.toml with a `package` table, package.json, or pyproject.toml). A Cargo `workspace` or `package` table counts however it is written: a header, an inline table, dotted keys, or, for `workspace`, a sub-table such as `[workspace.package]` alone."}
{"id": "WS-DISC-02", "criterion": "Root detection fails with E030 naming the start directory and Git toplevel when no marker or package manifest exists up to the toplevel, and with E058 (help suggests `git init`) when the start directory is not inside a Git repository.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-DISC-03", "criterion": "Discovery treats every directory under the root whose Cargo.toml, package.json or pyproject.toml parses and declares a package name as a candidate, following symlinks up to 32 levels deep (only so a package found beneath one can be named, see WS-DISC-11) and skipping hidden and gitignored paths and directories named target, node_modules, .git, .moon and dist; a manifest without a name is not a package. A manifest that fails to parse, or that cannot be read (a permission error, a directory, invalid UTF-8), is a coded error naming its path and the cause when the path is admitted by explicit workspace membership or shares a directory with an admitted manifest, otherwise it is a warning naming the path and the cause, and discovery continues without it. Only a package manifest that does not exist is treated as absent."}
{"id": "WS-DISC-04", "criterion": "A Cargo candidate is admitted when the root Cargo.toml [workspace] has no `members` key or its path matches `members`, and its path does not match `exclude`; Python candidates follow the same rules using pyproject.toml [tool.uv.workspace]. A glob entry written with a leading `./` or a trailing `/` (`./packages/*`, `packages/*/`) names the same directories as without. A workspace nested below the root (Cargo `[workspace]`, npm `workspaces` or `pnpm-workspace.yaml`, uv `[tool.uv.workspace]`) also judges the packages beneath it, through the nearest such workspace, by its own list; the workspace at the root keeps deciding for everything beneath it, and a nested manifest that cannot be read or parsed fails discovery. A private, versionless `package.json` that holds an npm workspace is never a package, at the root or below it."}
{"id": "WS-DISC-05", "criterion": "Membership globs match root-relative paths where `*` matches one path segment, `**` matches any depth, and an entry starting with `!` removes matching paths from the list's other entries."}
{"id": "WS-DISC-06", "criterion": "With no root manifest or no workspace table for an ecosystem, every candidate of that ecosystem is admitted; an empty `members`, `workspaces` or `packages` list admits none except a hybrid root."}
{"id": "WS-DISC-07", "criterion": "An npm candidate is admitted when its path matches the `packages` list of the root pnpm-workspace.yaml, or, when that file is absent, empty or has no `packages` key, the root package.json `workspaces` array; with neither, all npm candidates are admitted."}
{"id": "WS-DISC-08", "criterion": "A hybrid root (root Cargo.toml with both [package] and [workspace], root pyproject.toml with both [project] and [tool.uv.workspace], or a root package.json with a `name`) is always admitted, even when `exclude` matches `.` or the member globs do not. A root package.json that is both `private` and versionless is never discovered as an npm package (it only holds `workspaces`), regardless of admission. A Cargo crate with no `version` (an internal tool such as an xtask) is not discovered as a package and a note names it; a crate that inherits its version with `version.workspace = true` is."}
{"id": "WS-DISC-09", "criterion": "Malformed membership input fails discovery with a coded error naming the file and never falls back to admitting every directory: a root Cargo.toml, pyproject.toml, package.json or pnpm-workspace.yaml that cannot be read (E310) or parsed (E209), a `members`, `exclude`, `workspaces` or `packages` value that is not an array of strings (npm `workspaces` may also be an object with a `packages` array), a `[workspace]` that is not a table, a pnpm-workspace.yaml whose top level is not a mapping, a root package.json that is not an object, or an entry that is not a valid glob (E311). A missing manifest, a manifest with no workspace table or no `members`, and an empty or `packages`-less pnpm-workspace.yaml restrict nothing. A leading byte-order mark is ignored.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-DISC-10", "criterion": "An invalid glob in `members`, `exclude`, `workspaces` or `packages` fails discovery with E311 naming the file; no entry of the list is applied or skipped on its own.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-DISC-11", "criterion": "A package whose path passes through a symbolic link is a coded error naming the package (E312), because Git cannot stage a path beneath a link and a version bump there could never be committed; a symbolic link that holds no package is ignored, and a link can be ignored through a .gitignore under the workspace root.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-DISC-12", "criterion": "Locating the workspace root fails with a coded error naming the file when a Cargo.toml or package.json between the start directory and the Git root cannot be read (E310) or parsed (E209), instead of settling on a different root.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-LOAD-01", "criterion": "Loading a workspace for `version`, `release` or any command that writes fails with E313, naming each affected manifest once, when the graph build could not open a discovered manifest to read its platform role, publish targets or dependencies; it never continues with those packages omitted. This includes `status`, `add`, `pre`, `matrix`, `snapshot` and `compose-pr-body`.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-LOAD-02", "criterion": "A Cargo package belongs to the workspace its `package.workspace` names, else to the nearest `Cargo.toml` at or above its directory, never above the workspace root, that has a `workspace` table (the package itself counts); it need not be the root's. A `version.workspace = true` or `{ workspace = true }` dependency resolves against that workspace's `[workspace.package]` and `[workspace.dependencies]`, and a version or dependency spec rewrite is written to that workspace's manifest. A package that inherits when no Cargo.toml at or above it has a `workspace` table fails with E313 naming its manifest. A `package.workspace` that names a directory without a `[workspace]` or leaves the workspace root fails with E310, and an ancestor Cargo.toml that cannot be parsed fails with E209, each naming the file.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-ID-01", "criterion": "A package whose name is claimed by no other directory gets a bare id equal to its manifest name (for example `foo` or `@scope/foo`)."}
{"id": "WS-ID-02", "criterion": "A dual-manifest package (one directory with several admitted manifests) is one package whose id comes from Cargo.toml, else package.json, else pyproject.toml, and each manifest's own name still resolves to that package within its ecosystem -- including through `--package`, `add` and a changeset entry naming the package by a non-primary manifest's own name, not only its primary name."}
{"id": "WS-ID-03", "criterion": "When two or more directories declare the same name and no ecosystem declares it in more than one of them, each package is promoted to an ecosystem-qualified id, displayed as `cargo/foo`. Promotion preserves the package's key (its workspace-relative directory) and CHANGELOG path; `cargo/foo` selects it in both states. Newly created default tags use `{display id}@{version}`, so promotion changes their namespace. A promoted package's last tag is also found among `foo@*` tags whose commit contained a manifest of its own declaring the name `foo`, and none of another same-named package's. A bare `pre.json` `initialVersions` key moves to the one same-named package that existed when `pre.json` was added, else `version` fails with E245. A bare key whose only current package did not hold the name when `pre.json` was added pins nothing."}
{"id": "WS-ID-04", "criterion": "When two directories declare the same name in the same ecosystem (including a dual-manifest Cargo+npm `foo` and an npm-only `foo`), loading fails with E100 naming both paths.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-ID-05", "criterion": "A `[[fixed-group]]` or `[[linked-group]]` member written `cargo/foo` resolves to package `foo` when a Cargo manifest declares `foo`, promoted or not, through the same selector resolver used for `--package` and changesets; a member naming an ecosystem with no such manifest fails loading with E108. A member without its ecosystem fails loading with E317, except the native name of a platform package. A group that names one package twice, under the same spelling or under two of its native names, fails loading with E250.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-ID-06", "criterion": "A changeset entry naming the bare name of promoted packages fails `version` with E103 listing every qualified candidate and showing the qualified form to write, and `status` reports it as an error diagnostic. `callisto add --package` and `callisto matrix --package` reject any bare name with E317 and a qualified name no package has with E102. A changeset entry naming a package that does not exist is a warning in `version`, and the changeset is not consumed.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-ID-07", "criterion": "A dependency edge is created only to a workspace package that declares the dependency's name in the depending manifest's own ecosystem; a name declared only in other ecosystems creates no edge and one `unknown-package` warning listing the candidates (`cargo/foo`), and an ordinary name no workspace package declares creates no edge and no diagnostic. An explicit pnpm `workspace:` reference that cannot resolve to an admitted npm member creates no edge and emits one `unknown-package` warning."}
{"id": "WS-ID-10", "criterion": "For pnpm dependencies, Callisto preserves the declared key, target distribution name and exact `workspace:` suffix separately. `workspace:`, `workspace:*`, `workspace:^` and `workspace:~` are version-derived shorthands; aliases such as `workspace:foo@^1` resolve `foo` while retaining the declared key; relative references resolve only to the admitted npm member at the resolved directory. Valid npm `||` alternatives are evaluated as a union for dependency coverage, while any invalid alternative makes coverage unknown. Catalog and opaque references are not guessed or rewritten."}
{"id": "WS-ID-11", "criterion": "A package that uses the default tag template takes a `{name}@{version}` tag as its release only if the tag's commit did not hold a manifest of another ecosystem under that name; a package renamed into a name, or added beside a package that owned it, takes none of that package's tags or `pre.json` baseline. A commit with no manifest under the name is no evidence, so the tag is taken. `status` reports each rejected tag as an info diagnostic naming `previous-tag-templates`, and a version whose only tag was rejected is still planned for release.", "is_error_case": false}
{"id": "WS-ID-12", "criterion": "A package reference written in the colon form (`cargo:foo`) is rejected with E316 naming the slash form (`cargo/foo`) wherever a package is named: `--package` on `add`, `matrix` and `release`, changeset entries, `pre.json` keys, group members, `[[package]]` and `[[package-set]]` `match`, `product-package` and `[[release.artifact]] package`. The slash form is unaffected.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-ID-13", "criterion": "A package named in `callisto.toml` without its ecosystem is rejected with E317 showing the `<ecosystem>/<name>` form: group members (except the native name of a platform package), `[[package]]` and `[[package-set]]` `match`, `product-package` and `[[release.artifact]] package`, and likewise `add --package` and `matrix --package`. Changeset entries and `pre.json` keys keep their native names.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-ID-08", "criterion": "A package whose explicit `tag-template` equals another package's default tag template makes `status` and `version` fail with E101 naming both packages and the template.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-ID-09", "criterion": "A discovered package whose canonical manifests use different version grammars fails with E007 before version planning or init writes. This includes co-located Cargo.toml and pyproject.toml, whether the Python version is static or declared dynamic; matching numeric version strings do not bypass the grammar check. The error lists the grammars in a fixed order and its help says to put the Python package in its own directory.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-CFG-01", "criterion": "For each package, the first `[[package]]` rule whose ecosystem the package actually has a manifest in and whose name matches applies; a rule for an ecosystem the package has no manifest in never applies, even to a same-named package in another ecosystem."}
{"id": "WS-CFG-04", "criterion": "A `[[package-set]]` rule applies only when no `[[package]]` rule matches, the first matching set in declaration order wins, its `match` must start with `cargo/`, `npm/` or `pypi/` (a bare pattern is E317, the colon form E316), and the glob is compared with the package's name in that ecosystem."}
{"id": "WS-CFG-05", "criterion": "A matched rule overrides only the fields it sets; otherwise a package uses release trigger `changeset`, `CHANGELOG.md` in its directory (a configured changelog is relative to the package directory), and the publish targets its manifests declare."}
{"id": "WS-CFG-06", "criterion": "A `publish-to` override whose target ecosystem is not among the matched package's manifest ecosystems fails loading with E119.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-CFG-07", "criterion": "A `[[package-set]]` that matches no package emits one `package-set-matched-nothing` warning naming its pattern; a set matching a package that a `[[package]]` rule shadows counts as matched.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-CFG-09", "criterion": "A `[[package]]` rule that matches no package emits one `package-rule-matched-nothing` warning naming the rule.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-PLAT-01", "criterion": "A package.json with non-empty `os` and `cpu` arrays is an npm platform package whose platform and arch are the first entries; for Linux its ABI is `gnueabihf`, `gnu` or `musl` from the name's `-<abi>` suffix, otherwise none."}
{"id": "WS-PLAT-02", "criterion": "An npm-only platform package named in the `optionalDependencies` of exactly one admitted npm package becomes an attached platform package of that owner, inside or outside the npm workspace globs: it is not its own package and adds no publish targets to the owner."}
{"id": "WS-PLAT-03", "criterion": "A co-located platform manifest belongs to its directory's package, is never attached to another owner, and a `[[fixed-group]]` member may name any platform manifest by its own npm name."}
{"id": "WS-PLAT-04", "criterion": "When an owner is versioned or snapshotted, each attached platform manifest receives the owner's new version and the owner's `optionalDependencies` pins update to it, with no fixed group required."}
{"id": "WS-PLAT-05", "criterion": "An admitted platform package named in no owner's or several owners' `optionalDependencies` stays its own package and emits a `platform-package-without-owner` warning with its manifest path and the owner count or paths.", "is_error_case": true}
{"id": "WS-PLAT-06", "criterion": "A platform package outside the npm workspace named by several owners emits `platform-package-without-owner` stating it is not released; one named by no owner stays undiscovered with no diagnostic.", "is_error_case": true}
